## Required correction

### P2: Distinguish command collisions from duplicate autocomplete entries

`assessment-1.md:207,245` says issue #121 reproduces in Atomic, citing extension-command dispatch precedence. That establishes which command executes, not whether autocomplete displays duplicate entries.

Atomic’s default interactive path isolates the engine and disables extension loading in the terminal host. Its autocomplete merger explicitly suppresses remote commands whose names already exist locally and deduplicates remote names:

- [Default isolated-host selection](https://github.com/bastani-inc/atomic/blob/3342c8d3648065d1568579dbe44baf534feddf5e/packages/coding-agent/src/main.ts#L350-L353)
- [Host disables extensions](https://github.com/bastani-inc/atomic/blob/3342c8d3648065d1568579dbe44baf534feddf5e/packages/coding-agent/src/main.ts#L596-L604)
- [Name-based autocomplete deduplication](https://github.com/bastani-inc/atomic/blob/3342c8d3648065d1568579dbe44baf534feddf5e/packages/coding-agent/src/modes/interactive/interactive-autocomplete.ts#L163-L188)
- [Templates participate in that deduplication](https://github.com/bastani-inc/atomic/blob/3342c8d3648065d1568579dbe44baf534feddf5e/packages/coding-agent/src/modes/interactive/interactive-autocomplete.ts#L312-L339)

Bigpowers nevertheless registers competing commands at [omp-hooks.ts:252-260](https://github.com/danielvm-git/bigpowers/blob/cbff374ee2d4095b53a81696262a39f164fa0774/extensions/omp-hooks.ts#L252-L260). The collision and differing invocation bodies remain legitimate concerns.

**Correction:** describe default Atomic behavior as a command-ownership collision with autocomplete deduplication, not a demonstrated reproduction of Pi’s duplicate-entry symptom. Separate isolated-host, in-process, and command-catalog behavior. Preserve the one-slash-owner recommendation and make host-specific runtime checks future validation items.

## Non-blocking clarification

### P3: Check authorization for each action without necessarily asking again

`assessment-1.md:193,227` mandates a human-input gate before every delivery action. Distinct action authority is appropriate, but an existing explicit authorization may cover several actions. Atomic says not to seek approval again for already-authorized work: [question-tool policy:62-66](https://github.com/bastani-inc/atomic/blob/3342c8d3648065d1568579dbe44baf534feddf5e/packages/coding-agent/src/core/tools/ask-user-question/ask-user-question.ts#L62-L66).

**Clarification:** require each action to fall within explicit authorization; ask when authority is absent or scope changes, while preserving genuinely required approval gates. Keep the current research-only prohibition distinct from future operational policy.

## Findings that withstand review

No primary reclassification is requested. Full-source borderline probes support the distinctions between self-audit and independent review, reusable delegation guidance and orchestration, module-interface design and frontend design, and repository Git hooks versus Atomic event interception.

The benchmark-control defect, CI false-success paths, security exclusions, state-owner conflicts, and TestPyPI “dry-run” publication are source-backed. The model policy matches Atomic’s dated eval tables and supplied catalog subset, separates measurement effort from production recommendations, and acknowledges benchmark limitations.

Runtime compatibility remains untested. That uncertainty is appropriate for this research-only report and is **not itself a rejection reason**. No source, settings, or generated files were modified.